\title[Knee Stability and Tail-Exchangeability]{Knee Stability Is Equivalent to Tail-Exchangeability:\\ Order Statistics, Galois Connections, and Count Bands}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 26, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A \emph{demand multiset} is a finite multiset $D$ of non-negative integers. Its
\emph{upper-tail counting function} $\tail_D(t)$ counts the demands strictly larger
than a threshold $t$. Its \emph{knee} at gate $g$, $\knee_D(g)$, is the least
threshold exceeded by at most $g$ demands. We prove that knees and tails form a
Galois connection, $\knee_D(g)\le t \iff \tail_D(t)\le g$, and that the knee at gate
$g$ is the $g$-th largest demand (indexed from $0$, and $0$ past the end).
From this we derive a complete answer to the question of when two demand
multisets look the same through their knees. Equal knees at every gate is
equivalent to equal tail functions, and also to equality of the sub-multisets of
\emph{positive} demands. The unguarded statement with equality of full multisets
is false. Equal knees on a gate window $a\le g<a+w$ is equivalent to equality of
the tail functions after clamping to the count band $[a,a+w]$. We show that the
naive closed-band formulation fails. A sweep over $\max(|D|,|D'|)$ gates decides
all-gate equality. We also prove the conjugate-partition duality between knees and
tails, the area identity $\sum_{g<|D|}\knee_D(g)=\sum D$, and a sharp quantitative
stability theorem. A $k$-gate shift between knee profiles is equivalent to a
uniform additive bound $k$ between tails. This gives contamination and merge
bounds. The practical consequence is that a single tail histogram per collection
is a sufficient statistic for every question about knees.
\end{abstract}

